      <h2>Secular Places</h2>
      <p>Secular places can still offer comfort, reflection, and a sense of belonging.</p>
      <p>They may not be religious in the traditional sense, but they can still create space for quiet thought, shared human experience, and contemplation.</p>
      <p>These places remind us that meaning does not depend only on formal worship. It can also emerge in ordinary settings where people gather, reflect, and live together.</p>
      <p>The greatest incentive for spiritual assistance is often a genuine need for connection, guidance, and remembrance.</p>
      <p>Trump never a religious man worshipped only his father. On his desk in the oval office while he was president was a portrait of his father. While alive, Fred Trump boosted his son Donald and bailed him out when he ran into trouble. Probably Fred’s most impossible dream was for his son to become President of the United States.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
